            <div class="space-y-4">
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What's the difference between sleep apnea and sleep breathing disorder?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Sleep apnea is a specific type of sleep breathing disorder where breathing repeatedly stops. Sleep breathing disorders is a broader term that includes apnea, snoring, upper airway resistance, and other breathing abnormalities during sleep.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How are sleep breathing disorders diagnosed?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Diagnosis typically involves polysomnography (sleep study) that monitors breathing, oxygen levels, brain activity, and sleep stages throughout the night.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Are sleep breathing disorders hereditary?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">There is a genetic component to sleep breathing disorders. Factors like facial structure, airway anatomy, and obesity tendency can be inherited.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can children have sleep breathing disorders?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes, children can have sleep breathing disorders, often due to enlarged tonsils or adenoids. This can affect their growth, behavior, and academic performance.</div></details>
                <details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"><summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What happens if sleep breathing disorders go untreated?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary><div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Untreated sleep breathing disorders can lead to cardiovascular problems, daytime fatigue, cognitive impairment, and increased accident risk.</div></details>
